  "raw_response": "def solve(grid):\n    rows, cols = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n    \n    # Find the cell that has exactly 8 neighbors forming a ring of the same non-zero value\n    # The center is a unique color surrounded by 2s (or some repeated value)\n    \n    # Strategy: find a cell where all 8 neighbors are the same non-zero value\n    # Actually, let me look at examples more carefully.\n    \n    # Ex1: output has 4 at (6,1) surrounded by 2s. In input, 4 is at (6,1). \n    # The 2s in input are at (2,0),(2,5). Not adjacent to 4.\n    \n    # Let me think differently. Each non-zero value appears some number of times.\n    # Find the value that appears exactly once - that's the center.\n    # The surrounding value is 2 (appears in all examples).\n    \n    # Count occurrences\n    from collections import Counter\n    counts = Counter()\n    positions = {}\n    for r in range(rows):\n        for c in range(cols):\n            v = grid[r][c]\n            if v != 0:\n                counts[v] = counts.get(v, 0) + 1\n                if v not in positions:\n                    positions[v] = []\n                positions[v].append((r, c))\n    \n    # Find value appearing exactly once\n    singletons = [v for v, cnt in counts.items() if cnt == 1]\n    \n    # The center is the singleton, surrounded by 2s\n    # Ex1: singletons would be 4,3? Let me check. 4 appears once at (6,1). 3 appears at (8,8),(9,7) - twice.\n    # Ex2: 6 appears once at (2,7). Output center is 6. Yes.\n    # Ex3: 3 appears once at (8,6). Output center is 3. Yes.\n    \n    # But Ex1: 4 also appears once. Let me recount.\n    # Ex1 input: 4 at (6,1) only. Yes singleton.\n    \n    # So find the singleton that's the center, surrounded by 2.\n    # But which singleton if multiple? In all examples the surround value is 2.\n    \n    # Find the singleton value, place it centered with 2s around it.\n    \n    # If multiple singletons, pick the one that... let me just check test input.\n    # Test: count each value.\n    \n    center_val = None\n    center_pos = None\n    for v in singletons:\n        # Check: is this the center? The surrounding value is 2.\n        pos = positions[v][0]\n        center_val = v\n        center_pos = pos\n    \n    # If multiple singletons, we need to pick the right one.\n    # The surrounding is always 2. So pick singleton that isn't 2.\n    for v in singletons:\n        if v != 2:\n            # Could be it. Let me just pick based on: appears exactly once and isn't 2\n            center_val = v\n            center_pos = positions[v][0]\n    \n    # For test input, let me see: 9 appears once at (6,5), 1 appears at (9,0),(9,2) twice,\n    # 6 appears at (1,4),(7,6),(9,8) three times... let me just trust the logic.\n    \n    result = [[0]*cols for _ in range(rows)]\n    r, c = center_pos\n    for dr in range(-1, 2):\n        for dc in range(-1, 2):\n            nr, nc = r+dr, c+dc\n            if 0 <= nr < rows and 0 <= nc < cols:\n                if dr == 0 and dc == 0:\n                    result[nr][nc] = center_val\n                else:\n                    result[nr][nc] = 2\n    \n    return result",
